{"artifactHash":"sha256:e18306324e7f82e1ac9d315b578e001223a2da61765448c0af5f5a01e1702ae2","features":["core-executable"],"imports":[],"metadata":{"localSources":{"urn:openstax:clir:chemistry-bonding#OPENSTAX_CHEMISTRY_BONDING_TXT":"sources/chemistry-bonding/7.3.txt"}},"nodes":[{"contentHash":"sha256:bc6697f95c2580e696f7b2e72d740064aa899c24cb60c34b17d89163fbe27e5e","edition":"urn:openstax:clir:chemistry-bonding#OPENSTAX_CHEMISTRY_BONDING_SEC","fragmentKind":"defn","id":"urn:openstax:clir:chemistry-bonding#F_LEWIS_SYM","kind":"fragment","locator":"section/7.3#lewis-symbols","package":"urn:openstax:clir:chemistry-bonding","texts":[{"contentHash":"sha256:8c7dc1fe1d401b4d4e352a0d649957fca560b145e490291c5a6a16110e365f8a","language":"en","status":"official","text":"We use Lewis symbols to describe valence electron configurations of atoms and monatomic ions. A Lewis symbol consists of an elemental symbol surrounded by one dot for each of its valence electrons:"}]},{"contentHash":"sha256:e900c1af28e05a315a8e9c9c777391291ee0672e8f58ecb2530a3bd4e56f0d07","edition":"urn:openstax:clir:chemistry-bonding#OPENSTAX_CHEMISTRY_BONDING_SEC","fragmentKind":"paragraph","id":"urn:openstax:clir:chemistry-bonding#F_OCTET","kind":"fragment","locator":"section/7.3#octet-prediction","package":"urn:openstax:clir:chemistry-bonding","texts":[{"contentHash":"sha256:5358f25d88a9a6e7630b11e8dad2ca3dcbad4ddc1e493a5d6a6dc8fc66b7cabe","language":"en","status":"official","text":"The number of bonds that an atom can form can often be predicted from the number of electrons needed to reach an octet (eight valence electrons); this is especially true of the nonmetals of the second period of the periodic table (C, N, O, and F). For example, each atom of a group 14 element has four electrons in its outermost shell and therefore requires four more electrons to reach an octet. These four electrons can be gained by forming four covalent bonds, as illustrated here for carbon in CCl4 (carbon tetrachloride) and silicon in SiH4 (silane). Because hydrogen only needs two electrons to fill its valence shell, it is an exception to the octet rule. The transition elements and inner transition elements also do not follow the octet rule:"}]},{"contentHash":"sha256:767f0dbdb5432d9df508257411bcdecd69b3b02d8d077fce8d64eeb3ea68f5cf","edition":"urn:openstax:clir:chemistry-bonding#OPENSTAX_CHEMISTRY_BONDING_SEC","fragmentKind":"defn","id":"urn:openstax:clir:chemistry-bonding#F_SINGLE_BOND","kind":"fragment","locator":"section/7.3#single-bond","package":"urn:openstax:clir:chemistry-bonding","texts":[{"contentHash":"sha256:8922f7d44078e8890e06d4ea8723f5afff88ed97df90666f09a1fca151e5ebdf","language":"en","status":"official","text":"A single shared pair of electrons is called a single bond. Each Cl atom interacts with eight valence electrons: the six in the lone pairs and the two in the single bond."}]},{"anchors":["urn:openstax:clir:chemistry-bonding#F_LEWIS_SYM"],"body":{"items":[{"formula":{"args":[{"kind":"var","var":"v0"},{"kind":"value","type":{"name":"urn:law:std#Text"},"value":"calc.lewis_structures valence-count"}],"kind":"literal","polarity":"positive","predicate":"urn:openstax:clir:chemistry-bonding#row_checker"},"kind":"status","status":"established"},{"formula":{"args":[{"kind":"var","var":"v0"},{"kind":"value","type":{"name":"urn:law:std#Text"},"value":"section/7.3#lewis-symbols"}],"kind":"literal","polarity":"positive","predicate":"urn:openstax:clir:chemistry-bonding#row_fragment"},"kind":"status","status":"established"},{"formula":{"args":[{"kind":"var","var":"v0"},{"kind":"value","type":{"name":"urn:law:std#Text"},"value":"definition"}],"kind":"literal","polarity":"positive","predicate":"urn:openstax:clir:chemistry-bonding#row_layer"},"kind":"status","status":"established"},{"formula":{"args":[{"kind":"var","var":"v0"},{"kind":"value","type":{"name":"urn:law:std#Text"},"value":"Lewis symbol is the elemental symbol with one dot per valence electron"}],"kind":"literal","polarity":